<v Alex>How to test a new model on your own real tasks instead of looking at benchmarks, a fifteen-step one-prompt video edit, why borrowing old ideas from other industries works so well, and two Chinese threads on S E O automation and splash-screen ad metrics.

36
00:03:56.119 --> 00:04:09.420
<v Maya>简单说：今天聊网站该拦 Agent 还是迎 Agent、怎么用规矩对抗 A I 的“平均值长相”，以及怎么把同一个纠偏写进永久规则。

37
00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.537
<v Alex>Segment one. Top stories.

38
00:04:14.111 --> 00:04:21.512
<v Maya>Our first story is the centerpiece of today's show and the inspiration for Fisher's original post draft.

39
00:04:21.512 --> 00:04:27.745
<v Maya>It comes from Greg Isenberg, who has about seven hundred forty-three thousand followers on X.

40
00:04:27.745 --> 00:04:33.587
<v Maya>His post picked up six hundred ninety-two likes and four hundred twenty-four bookmarks.

41
00:04:34.235 --> 00:04:40.852
<v Alex>He opened with a bold claim: Delta, Chase, and Amazon are blocking A I agents.

42
00:04:40.852 --> 00:04:52.321
<v Alex>Now let's be completely upfront: whether those three companies are actively blocking agents comes strictly from his post—we have not independently verified that fact.

43
00:04:52.321 --> 00:04:58.055
<v Alex>And what follows in his post is his own forecast, not empirical data.

44
00:04:58.548 --> 00:04:58.958
<v Maya>Right.

45
00:04:58.958 --> 00:05:06.597
<v Maya>Based on that premise, Greg laid out nine stages of how the "Agent War" will play out across the internet.

46
00:05:06.597 --> 00:05:10.416
<v Maya>Let's walk through all nine stages step by step.

47
00:05:10.898 --> 00:05:15.249
<v Alex>Stage one: companies block A I agents. Why?

48
00:05:15.249 --> 00:05:21.230
<v Alex>Because agents skip right past display ads and ignore checkout upsells.

49
00:05:21.803 --> 00:05:29.528
<v Maya>Stage two: agents learn to disguise themselves as human users, turning the whole thing into an arms race.

50
00:05:29.987 --> 00:05:32.168
<v Alex>Stage three: the market splits.

51
00:05:32.168 --> 00:05:36.967
<v Alex>Companies that make money by actually selling goods let agents in.

52
00:05:36.967 --> 00:05:40.893
<v Alex>Companies that make money from data start charging fees.

53
00:05:40.893 --> 00:05:44.384
<v Alex>And the tech giants build their own agents.

54
00:05:44.841 --> 00:05:51.139
<v Maya>Stage four: a trusted middle layer emerges for agent identity and agent payments.

55
00:05:51.692 --> 00:05:58.589
<v Alex>Stage five: companies that welcome agents start stealing customers away from the companies that block them.

56
00:05:59.132 --> 00:06:07.080
<v Maya>Stage six: the blockers realize they are losing business, so they turn around and let agents in—on their own terms.

57
00:06:07.714 --> 00:06:13.608
<v Alex>And look closely at stages seven, eight, and nine. What happens in stage seven?

58
00:06:14.184 --> 00:06:28.915
<v Maya>Stage seven: everyone starts competing specifically for agents—competing on price, competing on checkout speed, and doing S E O—search engine optimization—specifically for A I agents.

59
00:06:29.468 --> 00:06:36.857
<v Alex>Stage eight: advertising comes back, as merchants pay money to become your agent's number-one recommendation.

60
00:06:36.857 --> 00:06:45.115
<v Alex>And stage nine: selling to an A I agent becomes just as normal and routine as having a website.

61
00:06:45.664 --> 00:06:58.223
<v Maya>也就是说：大公司一开始可能会拦 Agent，但最后大家还是得回头做“给 Agent 看的 S E O”，让机器能顺畅地读懂你的页面。

62
00:06:58.723 --> 00:07:03.695
<v Alex>Why do stages seven and eight matter so much to Fisher right now?

63
00:07:04.147 --> 00:07:13.698
<v Maya>Because Fisher's entire starting point with G E O—Generative Engine Optimization—is making sure machines can clearly read and understand his pages first.

64
00:07:13.698 --> 00:07:24.394
<v Maya>What Greg describes in stages seven and eight is the exact game Fisher is already playing, and it means that lane is going to get much more crowded.

65
00:07:24.883 --> 00:07:28.965
<v Alex>So you don't need to wait for stage seven to arrive.

66
00:07:28.965 --> 00:07:38.491
<v Alex>Before a website owner even worries about whether to block agents or welcome them, the first job is making sure an agent can read the page without guessing.

67
00:07:39.079 --> 00:07:57.572
<v Maya>That connects directly to Fisher's library item D R oh seven—the rule that A I only cites pages it dares to trust—and library item E V oh two, which notes that on his site with zero ad spend, A I assistants are already the third largest traffic source.

68
00:07:58.062 --> 00:08:14.016
<v Alex>And remember our hard rule whenever Fisher uses E V oh two in public: only talk about the relative ranking or general standing of traffic sources, never absolute traffic numbers or percentages, and use general examples.

69
00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:36.726
<v Maya>The daily notes recommend making this today's original post under the theme: "In the future your site will be read by agents before humans: whether you block them or welcome them, make your pages easy to read today." And if Fisher decides not to publish that as an original post, he can turn the short version into a direct reply to Greg.

70
00:08:37.177 --> 00:08:48.949
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: whether big brands block agents today or not, you win right now by formatting your pages so an agent can grab the exact answer without guessing.

71
00:08:49.977 --> 00:09:00.868
<v Maya>一句话：拦不拦 Agent 是以后的事，先把页面写成每一节回答一个清楚的小问题，让机器今天就能读懂。

72
00:09:02.918 --> 00:09:10.944
<v Alex>Let's connect that right away to story six, which looks at how A I search handles commercial products and vendor recommendations.

73
00:09:10.944 --> 00:09:22.618
<v Alex>This pairs a post from Brodie Clark in Australia, who has sixty-eight thousand followers, with a post from Lily Ray in New York, who has one hundred twenty-four thousand followers.

74
00:09:23.099 --> 00:09:29.844
<v Maya>Brodie asked a big question: what kinds of brands are going to skyrocket inside A I search?

75
00:09:29.844 --> 00:09:32.225
<v Maya>His answer is e-commerce brands.

76
00:09:32.729 --> 00:09:36.812
<v Alex>Why does he think e-commerce has the biggest advantage in A I search?

77
00:09:37.439 --> 00:09:42.179
<v Maya>Because of product feeds! A product feed is already clean, structured data.

78
00:09:42.179 --> 00:09:50.869
<v Maya>Brodie pointed out that Google A I Mode, Chat G P T, and Bing all prefer that exact same structured data format.

79
00:09:50.869 --> 00:10:00.349
<v Maya>Yet many e-commerce stores still haven't even submitted their product feeds through the ad backends of Chat G P T or Bing.

80
00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:06.462
<v Alex>What about Lily Ray's post? What did she spot inside G P T six?

81
00:10:07.082 --> 00:10:27.073
<v Maya>Lily Ray said she is seeing G P T six increasingly add disclaimers in its answers teaching users "how to select vendors." Now, she shared a screenshot from a tool called peec underscore a i, and since we did not view the image in our text read, we only know that one sentence from her post.

82
00:10:27.651 --> 00:10:30.397
<v Alex>Put Brodie Clark and Lily Ray together.

83
00:10:30.397 --> 00:10:37.065
<v Alex>On one side, A I search engines love fixed, structured fields that look like a product feed.

84
00:10:37.065 --> 00:10:46.088
<v Alex>On the other side, when people ask for recommendations, G P T six is reminding them how to check and verify a vendor.

85
00:10:46.504 --> 00:11:00.591
<v Maya>简单说：A I 搜索最喜欢字段固定的结构化信息；而当模型开始教用户“怎么挑供应商”时，谁的页面更好核实，谁就占便宜。

86
00:11:01.059 --> 00:11:10.375
<v Alex>And how does that apply to a site that isn't a traditional e-commerce store—say, travel guides or e-S I M pages that have fixed facts?

87
00:11:10.933 --> 00:11:22.243
<v Maya>Even if you don't have a shopping cart feed, you can put your core facts in the exact same spot on every page so a machine can read them like a feed.

88
00:11:22.243 --> 00:11:35.266
<v Maya>And for Lily Ray's point, it maps straight to Fisher's library item D R oh three: putting how the information was verified, the official source, and the verification date at the bottom of every single page.

89
00:11:35.818 --> 00:11:40.852
<v Alex>The daily notes say there's no need to write a standalone post here.

90
00:11:40.852 --> 00:11:53.077
<v Alex>Instead, we have sharp reply drafts for both Lily Ray and Brodie Clark in Segment four—and since Brodie's account is a bit smaller, a reply there is more likely to be seen.

91
00:11:53.701 --> 00:12:04.582
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: organize your recurring facts like a structured product feed, and stamp every page with its official source and verification date.

92
00:12:05.804 --> 00:12:17.482
<v Maya>一句话：把常驻事实固定在页面同一位置，并在页底写明官方来源和核实日期，同时讨好机器和真人读者。

93
00:12:19.532 --> 00:12:26.835
<v Alex>Now let's move to story two: why everything made by A I is starting to look identical.

94
00:12:26.835 --> 00:12:42.658
<v Alex>This pairs a Chinese note from shao underscore underscore meng, who has thirty-six thousand followers, summarizing a talk by Stripe's Chief Design Officer, lil underscore dill, alongside a study from DesignArena, which has twenty-five thousand followers.

95
00:12:43.228 --> 00:12:49.320
<v Maya>First, let's note our source boundary: we did not watch the video of the Stripe talk.

96
00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:54.695
<v Maya>Everything we have comes from shao underscore underscore meng's second-hand notes on X.

97
00:12:55.156 --> 00:13:00.801
<v Alex>According to those notes, what were the three warning signs that Stripe's design chief called out?

98
00:13:01.328 --> 00:13:06.361
<v Maya>Warning sign one: large language models naturally pull everything toward the average.

99
00:13:06.361 --> 00:13:16.007
<v Maya>Warning sign two: A I makes things "look finished" very quickly, but looking polished is not the same as actually solving the problem.

100
00:13:16.007 --> 00:13:24.814
<v Maya>And warning sign three: because generating output is so easy now, nobody thinks about who is going to maintain it later.

101
00:13:25.329 --> 00:13:27.316
<v Alex>Those three points are spot on.

102
00:13:27.316 --> 00:13:31.621
<v Alex>What was her advice for fighting back against that pull toward the average?

103
00:13:32.101 --> 00:13:34.425
<v Maya>She gave three pieces of advice.

104
00:13:34.425 --> 00:13:42.173
<v Maya>First, have your own point of view before you start, otherwise the A I will hand you a generic answer.

105
00:13:42.173 --> 00:13:52.246
<v Maya>Second, bake your quality standards directly into the tools, because today a lot of user interfaces are being generated in rooms where no designer is present.

106
00:13:52.246 --> 00:14:01.930
<v Maya>And third, never confuse "done" with "good"—a real human still needs to walk through the whole experience from start to finish like a user.

107
00:14:02.544 --> 00:14:05.761
<v Alex>And a post from DesignArena fits her warning well.

108
00:14:05.761 --> 00:14:10.765
<v Alex>Their post got two hundred eighty-one likes and one hundred ninety-seven bookmarks.

109
00:14:10.765 --> 00:14:12.195
<v Alex>What did they do?

110
00:14:12.721 --> 00:14:22.283
<v Maya>They analyzed more than sixty-four thousand websites generated by A I models and mapped out the distinct visual style of each model!

111
00:14:22.929 --> 00:14:25.827
<v Alex>Tell us what each model's default style looks like.

112
00:14:26.395 --> 00:14:33.894
<v Maya>According to DesignArena, Open A I leans toward cream backgrounds paired with italic serif fonts.

113
00:14:33.894 --> 00:14:38.394
<v Maya>Anthropic leans toward dark backgrounds with glowing purple accents.

114
00:14:38.394 --> 00:14:43.393
<v Maya>And Google leans toward navy blue dashboards with a sidebar!

115
00:14:43.964 --> 00:14:44.414
<v Alex>Ha!

116
00:14:44.414 --> 00:14:55.749
<v Alex>Once you hear that—cream and italic serif for Open A I, dark glowing purple for Anthropic, navy sidebar dashboard for Google—you can never unsee it.

117
00:14:56.152 --> 00:15:10.535
<v Maya>也就是说：每个大模型都有自己默认的“平均值长相”，不管是设计页面还是写文章，如果你不先立规矩，用户一眼就能看出是哪个模型生成的。

118
00:15:11.121 --> 00:15:14.886
<v Alex>And look at how directly this connects to Fisher's own work.

119
00:15:14.886 --> 00:15:18.338
<v Alex>Remember, Fisher builds with A I and does not write code.

120
00:15:18.338 --> 00:15:21.789
<v Alex>He already has a rule for this on the writing side.

121
00:15:22.269 --> 00:15:22.716
<v Maya>Right!

122
00:15:22.716 --> 00:15:32.300
<v Maya>Just like Anthropic loves dark purple and Open A I loves cream serif fonts, every model has its favorite robotic phrases in writing.

123
00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:38.967
<v Maya>If a reader spots those phrases and realizes a model wrote the page, trust drops immediately.

124
00:15:39.505 --> 00:15:56.828
<v Alex>So for a writing angle, the daily notes suggest combining Fisher's library item D R oh five—his rules for A I assistants—with W M P six a, which covers lessons learned from running multiple A I assistants at the same time.

125
00:15:56.828 --> 00:16:02.734
<v Alex>The theme: "A I's default look is the average; your rules are the difference."

126
00:16:03.231 --> 00:16:06.967
<v Maya>Keep the examples general and never say Fisher writes code.

127
00:16:06.967 --> 00:16:19.293
<v Maya>Also, the notes mention that the original video of the Stripe design talk is well worth Fisher watching when he has time, and we have a reply draft for DesignArena in Segment four.

128
00:16:19.824 --> 00:16:29.192
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: don't let A I hand you its default average style—write your own taste and banned defaults directly into your rules.

129
00:16:30.124 --> 00:16:41.184
<v Maya>一句话：大模型天生往平均值走，只有把你的标准和禁用项写进规矩里，做出来的东西才不会千篇一律。

130
00:16:43.234 --> 00:16:50.477
<v Alex>Segment two. A I workflows: handoff templates, review rules, and real-task testing.

131
00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:58.917
<v Maya>Story three is a follow-up from Eric Siu in Los Angeles, who has about forty-six thousand followers.

132
00:16:58.917 --> 00:17:05.894
<v Maya>Yesterday we talked about his Outreach Agent that prepares email drafts every day without ever pressing send.

133
00:17:06.387 --> 00:17:09.848
<v Alex>Today, Eric took that philosophy a step further.

134
00:17:09.848 --> 00:17:23.693
<v Alex>He announced that his company, Single Grain, turned five of the internal bots they actually run into public templates inside Skills Dojo, and he posted a second link with all five templates.

135
00:17:24.277 --> 00:17:29.423
<v Maya>Each template includes the bot's job description, its skills, and its daily schedule.

136
00:17:29.423 --> 00:17:37.878
<v Maya>And Eric highlighted the single most important thing to look at in every template: "Where the bot stops, and where you take over."

137
00:17:38.388 --> 00:17:44.821
<v Alex>Let's go through all five of his bots and see where the bot stops and where the human takes over.

138
00:17:44.821 --> 00:17:46.353
<v Alex>What is bot number one?

139
00:17:46.978 --> 00:17:49.907
<v Maya>Bot one is a pre-meeting intelligence bot.

140
00:17:49.907 --> 00:17:54.301
<v Maya>It gives you a decision card and a follow-up email draft.

141
00:17:54.301 --> 00:17:57.597
<v Maya>Whether to send that email is up to you.

142
00:17:58.179 --> 00:17:59.363
<v Alex>What is bot number two?

143
00:17:59.839 --> 00:18:06.302
<v Maya>Bot two is the outreach bot from yesterday: it lines up a small batch of emails every day.

144
00:18:06.302 --> 00:18:09.533
<v Maya>Nothing goes out until you approve the whole batch.

145
00:18:09.963 --> 00:18:10.942
<v Alex>Bot number three?

146
00:18:11.581 --> 00:18:17.731
<v Maya>Bot three is a short-form video bot: it picks four clips from a podcast, and you choose two.

147
00:18:18.362 --> 00:18:19.859
<v Alex>And bots four and five?

148
00:18:20.423 --> 00:18:27.438
<v Maya>Bot four is a hiring bot: it scores job applicants, but never sends an automated reply.

149
00:18:27.438 --> 00:18:35.768
<v Maya>And bot five is a sponsorship bot: when someone asks about pricing, it sends only one fixed first reply.

150
00:18:35.768 --> 00:18:38.837
<v Maya>Every exception is handled by a human.

151
00:18:39.416 --> 00:18:51.271
<v Maya>简单说：Eric Siu 把公司里用的五个 Bot 做成了模板，每个模板最核心的一栏就是写清楚“Bot 在哪一步停下、人在哪一步接手”。

152
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:54.815
<v Alex>Look at why that matters for Fisher.

153
00:18:54.815 --> 00:19:02.671
<v Alex>Fisher's team already follows the exact same rule—assistants only write drafts, and Fisher presses send himself.

154
00:19:02.671 --> 00:19:10.527
<v Alex>What Eric adds here is putting that exact handoff boundary into a clean template table for every bot.

155
00:19:11.162 --> 00:19:17.415
<v Maya>When you have multiple bots working together, you can't rely on memory to keep the boundaries straight.

156
00:19:17.415 --> 00:19:23.321
<v Maya>Writing down "where each bot stops" into a table or template makes the whole system much safer.

157
00:19:23.881 --> 00:19:34.832
<v Alex>For writing, the daily notes anchor this on library item E V oh eight—Fisher's three-bot system and correction log—and rule D R oh five.

158
00:19:34.832 --> 00:19:43.437
<v Alex>It can also serve as another clean example for version three of Fisher's C R M article, D R oh eight.

159
00:19:43.923 --> 00:19:58.471
<v Maya>And since yesterday's original post already used the "never press send" angle, today's reply draft to Eric shifts the focus to why you have to write the handoff point into the template instead of trusting people or bots to remember it.

160
00:19:59.097 --> 00:20:07.747
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: define every assistant's job by writing down the exact line where the bot stops and you step in.

161
00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:18.694
<v Maya>一句话：用多个助手干活时，不能光靠脑子记，要把每个助手“在哪一步停下”白纸黑字写进模板里。

162
00:20:20.744 --> 00:20:29.569
<v Alex>That brings us right into story four: how the Claude Code team reviews A I-written work and turns mistakes into rules.

163
00:20:29.569 --> 00:20:37.591
<v Alex>This comes from Prajwal Tomar, who has about fifty-seven thousand followers, summarizing an article from the Claude Code team.

164
00:20:38.155 --> 00:20:43.818
<v Maya>Let's state our source rule first: we did not read the original Claude Code article.

165
00:20:43.818 --> 00:20:48.773
<v Maya>All the numbers and practices here come from Prajwal's summary post on X.

166
00:20:49.299 --> 00:20:51.393
<v Alex>What numbers did he share from their summary?

167
00:20:51.925 --> 00:21:00.713
<v Maya>According to his post, Claude now writes about ninety percent of their code, and they no longer review every single line by hand.

168
00:21:00.713 --> 00:21:12.940
<v Maya>Between June and September, the number of changes they shipped more than doubled, while their rollback rate—how often they had to undo a bad change—dropped by more than forty percent!

169
00:21:13.438 --> 00:21:19.186
<v Alex>More changes, fewer rollbacks, and no line-by-line review. How did they do it?

170
00:21:19.643 --> 00:21:21.914
<v Maya>He listed five specific practices.

171
00:21:21.914 --> 00:21:28.725
<v Maya>Practice one: before submitting a change, have a second assistant review it with fresh eyes.

172
00:21:29.351 --> 00:21:31.510
<v Alex>Okay. What is practice two?

173
00:21:32.053 --> 00:21:41.380
<v Maya>Practice two: if the exact same review comment is given twice, write it into a permanent REVIEW file so the next review checks for it automatically.

174
00:21:41.939 --> 00:21:47.187
<v Alex>Say a correction twice, and it becomes a written rule in the review file.

175
00:21:47.187 --> 00:21:49.811
<v Alex>What are practices three, four, and five?

176
00:21:50.325 --> 00:21:59.269
<v Maya>Practice three: check small, low-risk changes quickly, and spend deep review time on changes where a mistake would cost money.

177
00:21:59.269 --> 00:22:11.194
<v Maya>Practice four: anything touching login, payments, or public release must always be approved by a human—a bot's approval can never replace a human's approval there.

178
00:22:11.194 --> 00:22:22.267
<v Maya>And practice five: put new changes behind a feature toggle to test internally first, roll them out gradually, and always keep a one-step rollback ready.

179
00:22:22.968 --> 00:22:37.335
<v Maya>也就是说：同一条审查意见只要说了两次，就立刻写进规则文件下次自动查；而凡是涉及花钱和对外发布的事，永远必须由真人批准。

180
00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:46.412
<v Alex>Now remember our hard rule: Fisher does not write code, and we never touch code details when Fisher writes or replies.

181
00:22:46.412 --> 00:22:49.697
<v Alex>Why is this story still useful for Fisher?

182
00:22:50.221 --> 00:23:09.642
<v Maya>Because the two rules that have nothing to do with code—"if you say the same feedback twice, write it into the rule file" and "anything that costs money or goes public requires human approval"—are the exact way Fisher builds his own rules folder in library item D R oh five and E V oh eight!

183
00:23:10.098 --> 00:23:35.819
<v Alex>For a writing angle, the daily notes suggest using D R oh five and E V oh eight around the theme: "How I set rules for my A I assistants: if I have to correct the same mistake twice, it becomes a written rule." And we have a reply draft to Prajwal in Segment four that focuses strictly on the non-code rule-making side.

184
00:23:36.293 --> 00:23:46.373
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: never repeat the same correction three times—the second time a bot makes the same mistake, lock the fix into its checklist.

185
00:23:47.717 --> 00:23:57.321
<v Maya>一句话：同一个错只要纠正了两次，就把它写成书面规矩，下次让审核助手自动对照检查。

186
00:23:59.371 --> 00:24:08.882
<v Alex>Next up is story five, from E X M seven seven seven seven, who has about one hundred fifty-three thousand followers.

187
00:24:08.882 --> 00:24:14.503
<v Alex>Yesterday we covered his seven rules for making Claude write like a human.

188
00:24:14.503 --> 00:24:17.530
<v Alex>Today he posted two more workflow gems.

189
00:24:18.111 --> 00:24:27.192
<v Maya>In his first post, he talked about how every single week there is a brand-new "frontier model" release, and everyone obsesses over benchmark scores.

190
00:24:27.192 --> 00:24:33.730
<v Maya>He said the only thing that actually matters is how the model fits into your own real work.

191
00:24:34.266 --> 00:24:37.676
<v Alex>How does he test whether a new model is worth switching to?

192
00:24:38.231 --> 00:24:44.548
<v Maya>He uses a simple three-step test. First: throw your own real daily tasks at it.

193
00:24:44.548 --> 00:24:52.443
<v Maya>Second: look for three to five specific places where it is noticeably better than the model you use right now.

194
00:24:52.443 --> 00:24:59.549
<v Maya>And third: keep pushing it harder and harder until it breaks, so you know where its ceiling is.

195
00:25:00.174 --> 00:25:01.842
<v Alex>And what was in his second post?

196
00:25:02.361 --> 00:25:10.988
<v Maya>He shared a workflow audit prompt—a prompt that tells the A I assistant to interview you about how you work before it suggests any automation.

197
00:25:10.988 --> 00:25:20.941
<v Maya>And look at the constraint he put inside the prompt: "Ask only two to three questions at a time, and do not move to the next step until I confirm."

198
00:25:21.509 --> 00:25:29.469
<v Alex>There is that human-in-the-loop pattern again: ask two or three questions, wait for my confirmation, then proceed.

199
00:25:29.469 --> 00:25:35.438
<v Alex>And look at his first post about testing models on real tasks instead of benchmarks.

200
00:25:36.025 --> 00:25:51.510
<v Maya>That is practically the exact answer to Fisher's library item W M P six b—how to judge whether a new A I tool is worth your time—and library item X oh seven, which is about assigning different models by task complexity!

201
00:25:52.031 --> 00:25:52.460
<v Alex>Right!

202
00:25:52.460 --> 00:26:02.065
<v Alex>In Fisher's setup, as documented in his model routing rules from October sixteenth, Argon writes the drafts while Grok reads X and searches.

203
00:26:02.065 --> 00:26:13.672
<v Alex>That split wasn't decided by reading public benchmark charts—it came from running them on Fisher's actual daily tasks and seeing which one did each job better.

204
00:26:14.059 --> 00:26:25.880
<v Maya>简单说：新模型值不值得换，别看网上的跑分榜，直接拿自己每天干的真活去试，按实际表现给不同模型分工。

205
00:26:26.483 --> 00:26:44.691
<v Alex>The daily notes mark this as today's backup idea for an original post: "I don't look at benchmarks; I look at which specific task it does better than what I have now." And we also have a direct reply draft to E X M seven seven seven seven in Segment four.

206
00:26:45.323 --> 00:26:53.550
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: ignore the weekly benchmark hype and assign models to jobs based on testing them on your own daily work.

207
00:26:54.730 --> 00:27:04.770
<v Maya>一句话：别看跑分看疗效，拿自己的日常任务去实测，哪个模型在哪件活上更强就让它干哪件。

208
00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:17.442
<v Alex>Let's close out Segment two with story eleven, a Chinese post from Shanghai by an account called vikingmute, who has about sixty-nine thousand followers.

209
00:27:18.079 --> 00:27:32.953
<v Maya>He posted that he connected a third-party M C P—Model Context Protocol connector—for Google Search Console to his Grok Bot, and handed total control of his website's S E O over to the bot!

210
00:27:33.404 --> 00:27:36.798
<v Alex>Wait—total control? What did he tell the bot to do?

211
00:27:37.399 --> 00:27:48.111
<v Maya>He set it up on a weekly schedule to observe the search data, spot changes, make a plan, modify the pages, and push the changes live to the website.

212
00:27:48.111 --> 00:28:03.627
<v Maya>In his words, roughly: "Just tell me after it's live." He also noted that a Google Search Console connector is a very practical need, and even though there is no official version yet, there are already plenty of third-party options.

213
00:28:03.627 --> 00:28:05.843
<v Maya>His post got twenty-one bookmarks.

214
00:28:06.455 --> 00:28:07.899
<v Alex>Okay, pause right there!

215
00:28:07.899 --> 00:28:16.921
<v Alex>"Modify the pages, push them live, and just tell me after it's done." That is the exact opposite of how Fisher runs his team.

216
00:28:17.398 --> 00:28:18.582
<v Maya>It really is!

217
00:28:18.582 --> 00:28:26.475
<v Maya>Now, having a bot automatically pull Google Search Console data every week, watch for changes, and draft an action plan?

218
00:28:26.475 --> 00:28:32.789
<v Maya>That part is super useful for Fisher's G E O and S E O work.

219
00:28:33.323 --> 00:28:37.571
<v Alex>Totally. Automating data observation saves a ton of time.

220
00:28:37.571 --> 00:28:41.819
<v Alex>Where do Fisher and vikingmute draw the line differently?

221
00:28:42.432 --> 00:28:46.676
<v Maya>On pushing changes straight to a live website without a human check.

222
00:28:46.676 --> 00:28:51.982
<v Maya>Fisher's team rule is that assistants only write drafts, and Fisher sends things himself.

223
00:28:51.982 --> 00:28:58.702
<v Maya>Whether he also keeps a human check before live page changes is one of today's things to confirm.

224
00:28:58.702 --> 00:29:01.885
<v Maya>We'll come back to it in segment four.

225
00:29:02.438 --> 00:29:15.039
<v Maya>也就是说：让 Bot 每周自动看 Search Console 数据、出计划非常省心，但直接修改上线这一步，留一道真人把关会踏实得多。

226
00:29:15.615 --> 00:29:31.141
<v Alex>The daily notes say no standalone post is needed here, and instead recommend using today's one daily Chinese reply slot to chat constructively with vikingmute about where each person draws that boundary.

227
00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:40.856
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: let bots automate all the search data monitoring you want, and keep a human check right before live pages change.

228
00:29:42.108 --> 00:29:50.028
<v Maya>一句话：数据观察和出方案可以全自动，改线上页面留一步人看最稳妥。

229
00:29:52.078 --> 00:29:52.880
<v Maya>Quick break.

230
00:29:52.880 --> 00:30:18.552
<v Maya>When we come back: Peter Yang on X's worsening reply-bot problem and why big-company resumes can be a "car wreck" in fast teams, Riley Brown's fifteen-step video edit from a single prompt, borrowing old tricks from other industries, why nobody in tech feels optimistic despite seeing fifty huge opportunities, and the ad K P I behind annoying splash screens.

231
00:30:19.247 --> 00:30:33.344
<v Maya>休息一下，马上回来聊怎么在满屏机器人回复里写出真人感、一句话剪完视频的十五个步骤，以及几个关于增长与行业心态的观察。

232
00:30:36.894 --> 00:30:43.467
<v Alex>Welcome back. Segment three. Growth mindset, A I media, and quick hits.

233
00:30:43.962 --> 00:30:51.297
<v Maya>Let's start Segment three with story seven, from Peter Yang, who has about two hundred eighty-nine thousand followers.

234
00:30:51.297 --> 00:30:54.598
<v Maya>He had two notable posts in the feed today.

235
00:30:55.088 --> 00:30:58.195
<v Alex>His first post got seven hundred ten likes.

236
00:30:58.195 --> 00:31:07.129
<v Alex>He said the reply-bot problem on X has gotten noticeably worse—and Elon Musk actually replied to him saying, "Working on it."

237
00:31:07.652 --> 00:31:15.767
<v Maya>And in his second post, Peter shared a clip from his newest podcast episode with Pomp, handle A Pompliano.

238
00:31:15.767 --> 00:31:20.038
<v Maya>Pomp talked about hiring people with impressive big-company resumes.

239
00:31:20.524 --> 00:31:26.573
<v Alex>How did Pomp describe hiring big-company people into a small, fast-moving team?

240
00:31:27.093 --> 00:31:29.683
<v Maya>He said it was "like a car wreck"!

241
00:31:29.683 --> 00:31:34.864
<v Maya>Because people from giant companies are used to having lots of resources and long, slow processes.

242
00:31:34.864 --> 00:31:39.721
<v Maya>Then you drop them into a team that has an idea at nine a.m.

243
00:31:39.721 --> 00:31:42.312
<v Maya>and ships it by ten thirty a.m.

244
00:31:42.312 --> 00:31:52.350
<v Maya>Pomp also mentioned that people ask A I financial questions they are too embarrassed to ask a human financial advisor, because they aren't afraid of the A I judging them.

245
00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:58.745
<v Alex>Let's zoom in on Peter's first post about X being flooded with reply bots.

246
00:31:58.745 --> 00:32:02.342
<v Alex>Why does that matter directly to Fisher's daily workflow?

247
00:32:02.907 --> 00:32:09.131
<v Maya>Because Fisher uses daily replies to build relationships and grow his presence on X!

248
00:32:09.131 --> 00:32:26.912
<v Maya>When the platform is flooded with automated reply bots, two things happen at once: first, a thoughtful reply that sounds like a real person stands out much more; second, everyone is suspicious, so generic replies immediately get dismissed as bots.

249
00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:32.819
<v Alex>So how do you prove in two sentences that you are a real practitioner and not a reply bot?

250
00:32:33.323 --> 00:32:42.301
<v Maya>By including a specific, lived detail from your own work this week—something a bot could never guess or make up from reading the original post!

251
00:32:43.042 --> 00:32:55.996
<v Maya>简单说：X 上的机器人回复越多，越不能说空话；在回复里带上一句只有你自己干过才知道的细节，比任何蓝标认证都管用。

252
00:32:56.535 --> 00:33:10.972
<v Alex>The daily notes say skip writing a standalone post on this, read the Pomp note as background, and reply to Peter's post about reply bots—without mentioning that Fisher uses assistants to draft his replies.

253
00:33:11.618 --> 00:33:20.080
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: beat the reply-bot noise by anchoring every reply in a real detail from what you actually built or tested.

254
00:33:21.333 --> 00:33:30.603
<v Maya>一句话：机器人能模仿语气但编不出你这周真干了什么，真细节就是最好的真人签名。

255
00:33:32.653 --> 00:33:37.718
<v Alex>Story eight is a wild look at where A I video editing is heading.

256
00:33:37.718 --> 00:33:46.401
<v Alex>It comes from Riley Brown in Brooklyn, who has about two hundred fifty-seven thousand followers, paired with a quick reaction from Peter Yang.

257
00:33:46.956 --> 00:34:00.177
<v Maya>Riley posted a video with the caption: "This video was edited with one prompt." The post got one hundred thirty-one bookmarks, and Peter Yang commented that Claude is insanely good at video editing.

258
00:34:00.806 --> 00:34:04.774
<v Alex>Hold on—one single prompt edited the entire video?

259
00:34:04.774 --> 00:34:10.505
<v Alex>What did the model actually do behind the scenes? Riley listed fifteen steps.

260
00:34:10.505 --> 00:34:12.268
<v Alex>Give us the highlights!

261
00:34:12.867 --> 00:34:18.802
<v Maya>He uploaded a raw video of himself talking, full of verbal stumbles and pauses.

262
00:34:18.802 --> 00:34:59.503
<v Maya>From that one prompt, the model analyzed the transcript, went onto X to find matching visual assets, cut out all the stumbles and dead air, built scenes inside a Blender template, took screenshots of its own work to self-check and fix mistakes, synced the camera angles and animations to every single word, added word-by-word captions, added sound effects and music, rendered three thousand four hundred eighty-seven frames in the cloud—which he claims took under twenty minutes and cost less than one dollar—and finally uploaded the finished video to Google Drive!

263
00:35:00.069 --> 00:35:10.322
<v Alex>Three thousand four hundred eighty-seven frames rendered in under twenty minutes for under a buck, plus self-checking its own Blender scenes via screenshots.

264
00:35:10.322 --> 00:35:13.193
<v Alex>Did Riley post anything else last night?

265
00:35:13.728 --> 00:35:23.253
<v Maya>Yes, he posted a short piece of advice for startups and content creators that got two hundred forty-three likes: "Don't be reverse-engineerable."

266
00:35:23.979 --> 00:35:37.880
<v Maya>也就是说：现在已经有人用一条提示词跑完找素材、剪口误、搭场景、自查渲染的十五步视频流程，不到二十分钟就能出成片。

267
00:35:38.412 --> 00:35:40.562
<v Alex>Why is this on Fisher's radar today?

268
00:35:41.196 --> 00:35:47.199
<v Maya>Right now, The Daily Signal is strictly audio-only, and Fisher's other content is written articles.

269
00:35:47.199 --> 00:35:56.027
<v Maya>If Fisher ever wants to turn the podcast or his articles into short videos later on, workflows like Riley's are already being shared openly.

270
00:35:56.027 --> 00:36:06.973
<v Maya>Riley said he is releasing a full breakdown video later this week, so the notes say no need to write or reply—just keep an eye out for his full tutorial.

271
00:36:07.437 --> 00:36:17.616
<v Alex>So the takeaway is: keep our focus on audio and text today, and bookmark one-prompt video workflows for when we want video distribution later.

272
00:36:18.592 --> 00:36:28.158
<v Maya>一句话：知道一句话剪视频已经能跑通全流程就行，等他这周发完整教程再按需了解。

273
00:36:30.208 --> 00:36:37.655
<v Alex>Now let's look at story nine, from Murat in Toronto—handle muratcan—who has about twenty-three thousand followers.

274
00:36:37.655 --> 00:36:45.102
<v Alex>His post only had two likes when we read it, which makes it a great quiet thread for a reply.

275
00:36:45.656 --> 00:36:49.394
<v Maya>He shared an experiment his team ran over the weekend.

276
00:36:49.394 --> 00:36:52.011
<v Maya>They were working on A I filmmaking.

277
00:36:52.011 --> 00:36:57.244
<v Maya>The question was how to keep characters looking the right way, with steady lighting.

278
00:36:57.884 --> 00:36:58.927
<v Alex>How did they solve it?

279
00:36:59.427 --> 00:37:05.546
<v Maya>They looked at how people building three-D animation engines for agents handle spatial layout.

280
00:37:05.546 --> 00:37:14.929
<v Maya>Before generating the video scene, they had the agent draw a top-down floor plan of where everyone stands and check it first.

281
00:37:14.929 --> 00:37:21.048
<v Maya>Eye lines, directions, and lighting came out much more stable than with text prompts alone.

282
00:37:21.659 --> 00:37:25.299
<v Alex>And what was Murat's big lesson from that weekend experiment?

283
00:37:25.884 --> 00:37:39.505
<v Maya>He called it "cross-profession transfer." When you are building with A I, borrowing a method that another industry already figured out is often much more effective than trying harder inside your own industry's habits.

284
00:37:40.198 --> 00:37:51.797
<v Maya>简单说：用 A I 遇到卡点时，去借别的行业早就想明白的老办法，往往比在自己行业里死磕提示词管用得多。

285
00:37:52.373 --> 00:37:54.269
<v Alex>And why does that matter for Fisher?

286
00:37:54.913 --> 00:37:57.940
<v Maya>Because that is Fisher's exact signature angle!

287
00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:05.128
<v Maya>Fisher takes growth methods from more than ten years of growth work and brings them to A I products.

288
00:38:05.645 --> 00:38:31.146
<v Alex>For writing, the daily notes connect this to library item W M P five b—how classic Airbnb growth tactics translate to today's A I tools—with the theme: "The best methods in the A I era are often old methods from another industry." And we have a clean reply draft for Murat in Segment four using a general growth example.

289
00:38:31.676 --> 00:38:40.812
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: don't reinvent the wheel inside A I—import proven playbooks from older fields like growth and animation.

290
00:38:42.002 --> 00:38:49.809
<v Maya>一句话：A I 时代最好用的招数，往往是从别的行业搬过来的成熟老办法。

291
00:38:51.859 --> 00:38:57.226
<v Alex>Story ten comes from staysaasy, an account with about seventeen thousand followers.

292
00:38:57.226 --> 00:39:03.487
<v Alex>It posted two sharp observations about the mood across the tech industry right now.

293
00:39:03.947 --> 00:39:10.861
<v Maya>The first post had a striking title: "Everyone sees opportunities and nobody is optimistic."

294
00:39:11.354 --> 00:39:17.736
<v Alex>Why would everyone see massive opportunities and still feel anxious and pessimistic?

295
00:39:18.369 --> 00:39:29.459
<v Maya>The post said the biggest difference between the A I wave and the earlier internet wave is that A I started with a massive conflict of interest from day one.

296
00:39:29.459 --> 00:39:35.374
<v Maya>Every company views the big foundation model labs as competitors that might eat their business alive.

297
00:39:35.374 --> 00:39:40.919
<v Maya>So anxiety is much higher, defense is much fiercer, and there are no real allies.

298
00:39:41.537 --> 00:39:45.056
<v Alex>And how did the post describe the opportunities people see?

299
00:39:45.528 --> 00:39:53.344
<v Maya>In the old days, people talked about seeing one to three opportunities that might give a ten-X return.

300
00:39:53.344 --> 00:40:02.804
<v Maya>Today, everyone sees fifty different opportunities that could be a thousand-X—except the probability of succeeding at any of them feels tiny!

301
00:40:02.804 --> 00:40:14.733
<v Maya>And the second post said every tech lead right now is building similar things: automated pull-request approvals, coding automation, A I cost optimization, and model switching and evaluation.

302
00:40:15.404 --> 00:40:27.936
<v Maya>也就是说：现在人人都看得到几十个看似能翻千倍的机会，但大家手头做的事其实高度同质化，所以普遍焦虑多于乐观。

303
00:40:28.482 --> 00:40:36.978
<v Alex>Look at that contrast: everyone sees fifty shiny opportunities, and meanwhile everyone is building the exact same tooling.

304
00:40:36.978 --> 00:40:41.698
<v Alex>How does a growth veteran like Fisher look at that?

305
00:40:42.267 --> 00:40:45.999
<v Maya>In growth, having fifty ideas was never the bottleneck!

306
00:40:45.999 --> 00:40:48.487
<v Maya>Tools are available to everyone now.

307
00:40:48.487 --> 00:40:59.684
<v Maya>What separates winners from everyone else isn't spotting fifty opportunities—it's picking one single channel or direction and going all the way deep on it.

308
00:41:00.266 --> 00:41:07.848
<v Alex>The daily notes say skip writing a standalone post here, and use a growth view to reply to staysaasy.

309
00:41:08.359 --> 00:41:17.783
<v Maya>So the takeaway is: more opportunities do not mean more directions—pick one channel and go deep while everyone else chases fifty things.

310
00:41:19.126 --> 00:41:27.165
<v Maya>一句话：机会多不等于方向多，找点子从来不难，难的是挑准一个渠道做透。

311
00:41:29.215 --> 00:41:38.661
<v Alex>Story twelve is a short, sharp Chinese post from sujingshen in Guangzhou, who has six thousand four hundred ninety-two followers.

312
00:41:38.661 --> 00:41:43.159
<v Alex>Yesterday we covered his product review of Tutti version two.

313
00:41:43.702 --> 00:41:52.923
<v Maya>Today he replied to someone about those super annoying mobile app splash-screen ads—specifically the "shake your phone" feature on splash-screen ads.

314
00:41:53.499 --> 00:41:59.495
<v Alex>Most phone users in China know these shake ads. What did sujingshen say is behind them?

315
00:42:00.072 --> 00:42:08.891
<v Maya>He said behind almost every "shake-to-open" splash screen sits a hard advertising revenue K P I—Key Performance Indicator.

